T MediumProfile                   | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 39-60   | The medium as the device reads it — extents, profiles and the coefficients already resolved into the working space. index and fits ozone to the Chappuis band, both against `02` §5's spectral projection over four hundred and seventy nanometres of wavelength domain — which is a host computation performed once per medium amendment, not a per-texel one. Deriving it on the device would be deriving it a million times. and that is where the width drops, at the store, which is `28` §1's Tier D declaration and not this profile's business.
F ProjectSphereDistance           | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 76-98   | The distance from a radius along a zenith cosine to a sphere of a declared radius. expanded form subtracts two nearly equal magnitudes at grazing angles and loses every significant digit exactly where the horizon is — which is the one place in the whole surface where the answer matters.
F AdvanceRadius                   | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 108-113 | The radius reached after advancing a declared distance along a ray. and taking its length instead would carry three coordinates through the march to recover one number that depends on none of them separately.
F ClassifyGroundReach             | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 119-127 | Whether a ray from a declared radius meets the planet before the atmosphere boundary.
F ProjectMarchDistance            | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 136-145 | The distance a ray runs before it leaves the medium — the ground where it descends, the boundary where it climbs. marches that disagree about where a ray ends disagree about the horizon, and ② would carry that disagreement into ③ as an ambient term rather than as a visible seam.
F ProjectOzoneDensity             | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 158-173 | The ozone tent's density at an altitude — unity at its centre, nothing beyond the declared half width. settles. That is exactly why it colours twilight and why the other two components do not: at a low sun the path crosses the tent nearly horizontally, and the Chappuis absorption it accumulates there is what leaves the zenith blue instead of grey.
F ResolveMediumExtinction         | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 182-195 | The medium's total extinction at an altitude, per component. fraction Mie absorbs, and dropping it makes a hazy atmosphere brighten rather than dim with depth — which reads as fog that glows.
F ResolveMediumScattering         | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 205-218 | The medium's scattering at an altitude, Rayleigh per component and Mie as one neutral magnitude. that appeared in this routine would be one that brightens the sky it is meant to tint. and a caller given only their sum cannot recover either.
F ProjectRayleighPhase            | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 229-232 | Rayleigh's phase, 3(1+cos²θ)/16π. every direction rather than only around the sun.
F ProjectMiePhase                 | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 246-255 | Cornette–Shanks, forward-biased by the declared asymmetry. better fit to Mie's forward lobe at the asymmetries an atmosphere uses. The two differ by the (1+cos²θ) factor and a normalisation; the visible consequence is the softness of the halo immediately around the sun, where Henyey–Greenstein is the blunter of the two. unity and a scatter cosine approaching it too, the base is a difference of two nearly equal magnitudes and reaches zero before the physics does; the power of a zero base is not a large magnitude, it is a non-finite one, and it propagates through the whole march rather than through one step.
F IntegrateStepInScatter          | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 275-284 | One march step's ∫₀ᵈ S·e^{−σₑs} ds, which is S(1 − e^{−σₑd})/σₑ. extinction coefficient at the top of the atmosphere is itself of the order any such floor would be, so a floor does not protect the division — it replaces the answer with a different one wherever the air is thin, which is most of the ray. step is optically thin, and the steps that are not are exactly the ones near the ground; the midpoint form's disagreement there is the banding a sunset shows across a smooth gradient.
F ProjectTransmittanceCoordinate  | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 298-308 | Where a declared radius and zenith cosine sit on ①. the horizon is the steep one, and the altitude the narrower because its gradient is not. Two hundred and fifty-six against sixty-four is that observation as a number — `Contract/` holds both.
F ProjectTransmittanceParameter   | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 315-322 | The inverse — what one texel centre of ① stands for. They are a pair and neither is correct alone.
F ProjectMultiScatterCoordinate   | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 334-339 | Where a declared radius and sun zenith cosine sit on ②. light came from — that is what makes it a thirty-two square surface rather than a directional one, and it is why ② survives every rotation the sun moves through without a rebuild.
F ProjectMultiScatterParameter    | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 344-350 | The inverse — what one texel centre of ② stands for.
F ProjectSkyViewDirection         | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 365-392 | The view direction one texel centre of ③ stands for. cosine. The horizon is where the radiance gradient is steep and where a linear parameterisation spends a handful of its hundred and eight texels, which reaches the artist as a banded sunset over a perfectly smooth zenith. Below the halfway coordinate the ray descends and above it climbs, with the horizon at the halfway point where both branches meet at zero departure.
F ProjectSkyViewCoordinate        | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 406-427 | Where a declared view direction sits on ③. would agree with the bake to whatever tolerance somebody measured; the square root agrees with it identically, which is the difference between one mapping and two that resemble each other. `ResidentSurface::Sample` clamps both of its axes and is right to: the zenith axis genuinely ends at the zenith, and a wrapped sample there reads the horizon, which appears as a bright ring directly overhead.
F ProjectLocalUp                  | Shared/AtmosphereProjection.slang.h   | 443-452 | The local up at a marched sample, as a direction from the planet centre. origin's. A ray that travels a hundred kilometres around a planet of six thousand has turned measurably under itself, and the error is largest at exactly the grazing angles where twilight is decided. the arrival is the origin displaced along the view direction, and dividing by the arrival's radius is what normalises it without a second square root.
F ClassifyOrdinalContainment      | Shared/ContainmentClassifier.slang.h  | 38-46   | Classifies one ordinal against a closed interval. so an occupant sitting exactly on its enclosure's bound is a label that was issued wrongly rather than an occupant that is enclosed — and reporting it as enclosed hides the defect that produced it.
F OrdinalEnrolled                 | Shared/ContainmentClassifier.slang.h  | 55-58   | Whether one ordinal is enrolled in a closed run. both ends while its interval labels nest strictly. Both spellings exist so that neither consumer has to remember to adjust the other's answer by one.
F ClassifyIntervalContainment     | Shared/ContainmentClassifier.slang.h  | 76-83   | Classifies one interval against another. for an occupant against itself without any consumer having to exclude it. Overlapping and inverted intervals both resolve to −1: neither is containment, and `12` invariant 4 forbids the first outright.
F IntervalsDisjoint               | Shared/ContainmentClassifier.slang.h  | 92-96   | Whether two intervals share no ordinal at all. takes. Over one ordering it holds exactly when each label begins after the one before it ended, which is why `SceneStructure` walks the ordering rather than comparing every pair.
