F ProjectDistributionGGX          | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 52-68   | The GGX microfacet distribution at one half-direction. surfaces carry. The tail is what makes a rough metal read as metal rather than as a matte solid, and it is precisely what a Gaussian distribution loses. differ by the order of one subtraction, and the subtraction is of two nearly equal magnitudes at grazing angles — where every silhouette is.
F ProjectDistributionAnisotropic  | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 82-106  | The GGX distribution stretched along the tangent by a declared anisotropy. a call into this because the isotropic denominator collapses to one squared term. Writing the isotropic case through the anisotropic form costs two divisions at every pixel of every rotation for the seven of `18` §3's eight selections that are isotropic.
F ProjectVisibilitySmith          | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 128-155 | The height-correlated Smith attenuation, already divided by the four cosines the reflectance carries. independent events and over-darkens at grazing angles by a factor that grows with roughness — which is a rough surface whose silhouette is darker than its interior for no physical reason. radicals below, so folding it here is one division instead of one division and a near-cancellation — and a caller that forgot it would produce a surface that is correct only where both cosines are unity. a path does not depend on which end the light entered from, and a lapse there is a surface that is brighter seen from one side than from the other.
F ProjectFresnelSchlick           | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 173-179 | Schlick's approximation of the Fresnel term, per component. It is exact where the general form is a logarithm and an exponential, and it is the one term of the direct reflectance evaluated at every pixel for every illuminant that reaches it. both checked by `ParityRunner`, because a Fresnel that is merely close at normal incidence is a material whose declared reflectance is not the reflectance it shows.
F ProjectDiffuseSingleScatter     | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 202-237 | The single-scatter diffuse lobe, unitless and already divided by the circle constant. integrates to unity at every roughness rather than gaining energy with it. The azimuth term is the residual of the two directions against the orientation, which avoids the arc cosines and the tangents the original formulation reaches for — and those are the two intrinsics the two toolchains agree on least well. `18` §2 declares the channel perceptual and §4 squares it **for the distribution**; the diffuse lobe's own roughness is the surface's slope variance, which is the perceptual figure directly. Squaring it here would make a material's diffuse and specular roughness respond to one slider at two rates.
F ProjectDiffuseMultiScatter      | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 254-267 | The multiple-scattering term the single-scatter lobe loses at high roughness. lobe lost is returned at the surface's own albedo, and that fraction is itself partly lost, and so on. The closed form is what the series sums to — the same reasoning `28` §2's multiple-scattering surface applies at a different scale. the specular term. The two differ in EON's own fitted coefficients, which are the donor formulation's and are not this document's to transcribe — recorded as an open row rather than guessed at. What the two share is the property `18` §4 actually asks for: energy correctness at high roughness, where Lambert has none.
F ProjectSheenDistributionCharlie | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 281-292 | The Charlie distribution — the fibre lobe `18` §3's Cloth selection reads. than falling away from the mirror direction. A GGX sheen produces a highlight where the light is, which is the one place a velvet does not have one.
F ProjectSheenVisibility          | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 300-313 | The neutral attenuation the fibre lobe is paired with. distribution is, and Charlie's has no closed Smith form; the neutral pairing is the donor formulation's own and is what keeps the cloth response bounded at grazing without inventing one.
F ProjectAlbedoCoordinate         | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 335-342 | Where a declared view angle and roughness sit on `18` §4.1's lookup. fastest where the surface is nearly smooth and barely at all where it is fully rough. A linear axis spends most of its texels on the half of the domain that is almost constant, and the artist meets that as a metal whose highlight steps rather than sweeps as they drag the slider through its first tenth. `Shared/`: the lookup is derived on the host and sampled on the device, so the mapping crosses the toolchain seam in both directions. A derivation that placed its samples through one arrangement and a sample that read them through another is wrong by half a texel everywhere — uniformly, so nothing looks broken and every metal is simply a little too dark.
F ProjectAlbedoParameter          | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 349-359 | The inverse — what one texel centre of the lookup stands for. They are a pair and neither is correct alone.
F ProjectMultiScatterCompensation | Shared/ReflectanceProjection.slang.h  | 375-385 | The factor multiple-scatter compensation applies to the specular term. `18` §3's selections but the last two. Single-scatter GGX loses energy at high roughness and the loss reads as rough metal being too dark — which an artist corrects by raising an albedo that was already correct, and the correction is then wrong at every other roughness.
K SlateRadicalDigitCeiling        | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 25      | ?
F ProjectRadicalTwo               | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 43-48   | The radical inverse of one ordinal in base two. and the scale is a change of exponent alone. This is the strongest determinism available anywhere in the engine, and it is why the first coordinate of every sample is the base-two one.
F ProjectRadicalThree             | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 61-81   | The radical inverse of one ordinal in base three. single division at the end is correctly rounded. Accumulating a fraction digit by digit instead would round twenty times, and the twenty roundings differ between two toolchains that reassociate. inverse. The result stays in the unit interval and stays deterministic; it merely repeats sooner.
F ProjectPlanarSample             | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 93-99   | Projects one sample ordinal onto the unit square, progressively. accumulates across rotations, so a pattern that is uniform only at a fixed count converges to a different value than the one `64` assumes it is averaging.
F ProjectPermutedOrdinal          | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 120-128 | Permutes one ordinal against a declared seed. the substitution both `54` §1 and `58` §6 name. The distinction is the whole point: a permutation of an ordinal reopens identically, agrees between a coarse reduction level and the finer one that replaces it, and agrees between `82`'s host preview and the device resolution. Sampled noise satisfies none of those, and each failure reaches the artist as the pattern changing when nothing was edited. exclusive-or whose shift is under half the width. A composition of bijections is a bijection, so the sequence visits every ordinal once before it repeats and no ordinal is favoured.
F ProjectVariation                | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 144-149 | Projects one ordinal onto the unit interval, through the permutation its seed selects. with the declaration. A stroke varying from an unrecorded source resolves differently every time it is re-resolved, so an undo would produce the inverse of a stroke the artist never made and `20`'s reconstruction of an evicted tile would disagree with what was on screen. divide the ordinal range favours its low residues, and the bias is a pattern leaning one way that no declaration asked for.
F ProjectSubPixelOffset           | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 169-181 | The sub-pixel offset one rotation slot carries. after resolution shifts an already-resolved image, which resamples rather than samples, and the result is blur where convergence was wanted. would place the first rotation of every run at a pixel corner rather than within the pixel. with `ParityRunner` like any other shared entry point — `64` §8's gate.
F ProjectSphericalSample          | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 201-215 | Projects a unit-square sample onto the sphere, uniformly in solid angle. projection uniform in solid angle. Distributing the angle uniformly clusters samples at the poles, and `28` §2 integrates its multiple-scattering surface over exactly this sphere. between two toolchains, so `ParityRunner` compares this against a declared bound in units in the last place rather than for equality.
F ProjectHemisphericalSample      | Shared/SampleProjection.slang.h       | 226-239 | Projects a unit-square sample onto the hemisphere, weighted to the cosine lobe. lift to the hemisphere is cosine-weighted by construction. Weighting after the fact by multiplying an evenly distributed direction by its own cosine is the same integral with more variance, and `60` §5 accumulates it over a target that is already half extent.
